A||||10||Richard Parnell||V8 Fiero 5th gear clutch slippage||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||02-08-2001||08:27 PM||||This is for all you other V8 Fiero owners.  There are 4 Fieros on the island of Oahu here.  Some have the V8 Archie kit some are different kits.  All of them the clutch slips in 5th gear when you really get on it.  Do any of you guys have this same thing going on?  If anyone has had this problem and figured out a fix let me know.  There are several of us that would like to know if this is just the nature of the beast or if we can eliminate this problem.  I am using Archies' supplied centerforce clutch and broke it in properly. Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Alex4mula||02-08-2001||10:11 PM||||MMmm?? That doesn't sound right. A V8 could be powerful but to slip in 5th by just acelerating?? That would require serious power, especially in that gear. I don't think that the amount of torque once engage could compare to a 3.4 V6 droping the clutch at 3.5K, which in this case (mine) doesn't slip. I think something else should be wrong. I have had the Centerforce DF and that baby grips very good. Even my C5 Vette doesn't seem to provide that brute torque in that gear. Only if downshifting.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||02-08-2001||10:24 PM||||Back off the timing advance. Disconnect the secondaries.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Sorry. Couldn't resist. <BR>I'm not too surprised that they slip. That's a bunch of horsepower through a relatively small clutch.<BR>When I first put my 350 in my Monza, I kept the original size clutch that came with the 262. Think it was 9". Even driving it easy (sorta), it glazed and fried in very short order. Had to replace it with the 10.5 that came with the 305. I suppose that is not an option with the Fiero.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||fieroshop||02-08-2001||11:15 PM||||The reason that we had clutches specially made for us is that no other clutch in the market would hold up.<BR>We also have a clutch specifically designed for the V8. This is a clutch that will withstand 550 HP. It is made from kevlar as my other clutch.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/V-8_Clutch.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||02-08-2001||11:15 PM||||Not only V8's do it. LMK if you find a cure.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_1.gif">  <BR>SC 3800||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||02-08-2001||11:17 PM||||Fiero shop we posted at the same time - correct me if I'm in error but that clutch doesn't look too "street friendly"||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroshop||02-08-2001||11:56 PM||||This clutch has been street driven and will hold up. It is a little more aggressive but the design was to hold up to higher HP and still be able to drive your car on the street.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Alex4mula||02-09-2001||09:00 AM||||I would strongly recommend any Fieroshop clutch. I have one from him in my 3.4 and that's the one I say doesn't slip. Give it a try.||||reg||1
Z||000008||FieroLT1||02-09-2001||10:28 AM||||Alright, let me pose this question then...<BR>Does it slip on all the other gears too? You'd almost think it has too. I know mine does. I can get my Centerforce to slip in every gear if I wanted to. (I don't though), but sometimes it bothers me that comming out of an on-ramp into the free way doing a good 40mph on 4th gear, I slam on the gas to catch up with oncomming traffic and the sucker takes a little while to finally stick. Is this a thing with the Centerforce only? I think they require a 500mi break-in period. I have about 800mi on it already, but I'd say about 500 of those are freeway miles, so I am giving it a little more time to get better. But I am gettting nervous already. What if it never stops sliping?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Winston Ojeda<BR>88 Formula LT1<BR>Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA<BR><A HREF="http://www.FieroLT1.com" TARGET=_blank>FieroLT1.com</A>||||reg||9
Z||000009||Richard Parnell||02-09-2001||01:29 PM||||Fiero LT1- It only slips in 5th gear.  I know, you would think that it would do it in all gears but I only notice it in 5th.  It sounds and feels like you're driving an automatic.  It's weird, the car is still accelerating but you can hear the rpms going up for a few seconds and then when it catches completely your outta there.  It doesn't bother me that much.  I'm a pretty mellow driver until someone in a vette or camaro gives me grief.  Then I just quietly drop it into 4th gear.  You know how the story goes.<BR>I'll let the other guys know about the kevlar clutch.  Looks kinda spooky to me.  Is that enough surface area to last very long?||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroshop||02-09-2001||03:37 PM||||There is enough material to hold up. The main reason that the clutch is like that is the less area on the disc the higher the compression ratio. I know it sounds weird but that is the explanation I recieved from the mfg.<P>------------------<BR> <BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by fieroshop (edited 02-09-2001).]||||reg||1
